# Execution model specification

# Status and authority

This document specifies how a modules.cpp program executes: what a portable
interface call may do with the caller's thread of control, what a provider
may do without it, and what an interrupt may and may not do. It states rules
the interface documents already apply one at a time, so that a new driver,
provider, or application is written against one contract rather than
inferred from several.

Each interface document owns its own calls and their answers:
docs/modules-platform-mcu.mdy owns mm.mcu and the GPIO edge latch,
docs/modules-display.mdy the display, docs/modules-stdio.mdy the console,
and docs/modules-touch.mdy, docs/modules-imu.mdy, and docs/modules-rtc.mdy
the device interfaces. docs/modules-c++20.mdy owns what code may say; this
document owns what code may do with time. Where the two overlap, the
language document's rules on exceptions, callbacks, and virtual dispatch are
the reason several of the rules below hold, and are cited rather than
restated.

Conformance language follows docs/modules-c++20.mdy: MUST and MUST NOT are
absolute, SHOULD needs a written reason to deviate, MAY is optional.

# Decision

A modules.cpp program is one thread of control that runs from main to
return, on one core, and every call it makes returns to it. Nothing runs
application code on the program's behalf: no callback, no handler, no
thread. Where hardware acts on its own, a provider may record that it did
in a latch, and the program asks the latch when it chooses.

There are two models, and the second is the first with one addition.

# Model 1: straight blocking execution

The program is the sequence it is written as: initialize the devices it
names, then loop, asking a device, computing, telling a device, and waiting.

Opening fence
auto& display = mm::display::selected_display();
if (display.initialize() != mm::display::Status::Ok) return 1;
for (;;) {
    std::size_t count = 0;
    if (touch.read(points, count) != mm::touch::Status::Ok) return 2;
    if (count != 0) compose(points[0]);
    if (display.write(area, frame) != mm::display::Status::Ok) return 3;
    if (display.refresh(mm::display::Refresh::Full) != mm::display::Status::Ok)
        return 4;
    if (mm::mcu::delay_ms(hold_ms) != mm::mcu::Status::Ok) return 5;
}
Closing fence

The rules that make this shape hold are these.

- Every call MUST return. A call completes its work or answers a Status; it
  does not wait on hardware for a time the caller did not bound. A wait a
  provider or driver makes on the caller's behalf MUST be bounded by a
  value the board's wiring declares, such as `busy_timeout_ms` and
  `reset_hold_ms` in the SSD1680 and CST328 wiring. When the bound is
  reached, the call MUST return rather than continue, and what it answers
  depends on what the bound was for: a wait for work the call had to
  finish -- a controller's busy line before a command it must send --
  answers `Timeout`, because the work did not happen; a wait that was the
  call's whole purpose -- `delay_ms` to its duration, `gpio_wait` to its
  deadline with nothing pending -- answers `Ok`, because it did. `Timeout`
  means required work did not finish, never that time passed.
- A call with nothing to report MUST answer `Ok` with an empty result, not
  an error and not a wait. `mm::touch::read` answers `Ok` with count zero
  when nothing is touching; `mm::stdio::read` answers `Ok` with count zero
  when no byte is waiting; `mm::mcu::gpio_take` answers `Ok` with pending
  false when no edge is held. Nothing being there is an answer.
- A query MUST NOT wait for state to change. `mm::stdio::connected` is the
  stated example: it reports whether a host is attached now, and a caller
  that wants to wait for one polls it and owns the stopping condition. The
  same holds for `geometry`, `board`, and every other call that reports
  state. A query MAY do work to produce its answer the first time it is
  asked, provided that work ends without any external event: the Linux MCU
  provider's `board` resolves the platform map on first use, which reads
  the override file the operator named. The map opens it without blocking,
  checks the descriptor it got is a regular file, and reads at most 64 KiB
  of it, so the read is bounded by the limit whatever the path becomes
  or the file does meanwhile. That is the one place a query does I/O.
- Time passes only where the program spends it. `mm::mcu::delay_ms` is the
  one call whose purpose is to let time pass; a transfer takes the time the
  transfer takes; and a bounded wait inside a driver takes at most its
  bound. A provider MUST NOT sleep, spin, or block in any other call, and
  a transfer whose far end may stall -- a console pipe whose reader has
  stopped reading, a terminal that has stopped draining, a socket whose
  peer has stopped reading -- is itself bounded: the Linux console writes
  such a far end through a nonblocking operation, gives up after a second,
  and reports the partial count its contract allows. A transfer whose far
  end is the machine -- the same console writing a regular file -- takes
  the time the file system takes, which is the transfer's own time and not
  a wait. docs/modules-platform-linux.mdy says which is which.
- Nothing runs behind the program. A provider MUST NOT change state the
  program can observe between two of the program's calls, except as the
  hardware itself changes it. What the program read is what was there when
  it asked; what it wrote is on the panel when `write` returned, or when
  `refresh` returned on a panel that needs one.
- An output parameter changes only on `Ok`. This is universal: a caller
  that sees a failure knows its own variables are as they were. Every
  interface document states it for its own calls; here it is the rule they
  are instances of, and a provider that computes an output as it goes
  keeps the running value aside and stores it only on the `Ok` path, as
  the Linux console provider does. `mm::stdio::write` is the one interface
  whose success answer is itself partial -- it reports how much it
  accepted, possibly zero, and the caller resumes at that offset -- and its
  document says so; that is a property of `Ok`, not an exception to the
  rule.
- A failed call MUST leave the provider's own record of the world true.
  Whether it leaves the hardware as it was is a promise each operation
  makes or does not, and the document of the operation says which: a
  failed `gpio_configure` or `gpio_watch` leaves no half-configured pin and
  no watch on one side of the seam, because each is one step the provider
  can undo; a failed SSD1680 `write` may have moved the controller's
  window and sent part of the data before a later transfer failed, because
  a multi-transfer command has no undo, and its document says the
  controller is then in the failed state its state machine records. The
  rule is not "no external effect on failure" -- transports do not offer
  that -- but "nothing the provider tells the caller afterwards is false".

A provider MAY be concurrent inside itself, and several are: the Pico SDK
services its USB interrupts under `mm.stdio`, the Linux display provider
blocks in the kernel under `write`, the SDL provider pumps a window. None
of that is visible above the seam. Each such call returns a Status to the
caller, on the caller's thread, before the caller's next statement runs.

Polling is therefore the native rhythm, and its cost is that a loop with
nothing to do still runs, and that the program cannot be told something
happened while it was in `delay_ms`. Model 2 is what the project does
about that.

# Model 2: the latch

The GPIO edge facility in mm.mcu -- `gpio_watch`, `gpio_take`,
`gpio_wait`, and `gpio_unwatch`, specified in docs/modules-platform-mcu.mdy
-- adds interrupts to model 1 without changing its shape. The rule is that
an interrupt may set a flag and may not run application code.

Opening fence
// A button on a pulled-up input: pressed is low, so a press is a falling
// edge. The loop sleeps until one arrives or a second passes, and a press
// that arrived while it was busy is held for it.
if (mm::mcu::gpio_watch(button_pin, mm::mcu::Pull::Up, mm::mcu::Edge::Falling)
    != mm::mcu::Status::Ok) return 1;
for (;;) {
    bool pressed = false;
    if (mm::mcu::gpio_wait(button_pin, 1000, pressed) != mm::mcu::Status::Ok)
        return 2;
    if (pressed) advance();
    show();
}
Closing fence

The edge is the whole event here: a press is a transition and the latch
reports transitions. It reports them without debounce, so a switch that
bounces may be two presses; a program that cares reads the level after a
short `delay_ms` and decides, as it would on any platform. A device whose
state persists between transitions -- a finger that stays down -- is not
read this way, and the next paragraph says how it is.

What stays and what changes:

- Control flow is still straight. The loop is the same loop. A step may be
  skipped because the latch says nothing happened, and `delay_ms` may be
  replaced by `gpio_wait`, which returns early when the edge arrives. The
  wake is something the program asked for, not something done to it.
- The handler belongs to the provider and is trivial. On the Pico it is the
  SDK's GPIO callback setting one flag and signalling an event; on Linux it
  is the kernel queueing an event on a descriptor. A handler MUST NOT call
  portable code, allocate, log, or touch a bus. No portable code has an
  interrupt context, so no driver need be re-entrant and no rule about
  priorities or preemption enters any portable contract.
- The latch coalesces. It reports at least one selected edge observed and
  not yet reported: no count, no order, no timestamp, no debounce. That is
  the whole promise, and it is what lets one contract hold on a one-flag
  microcontroller and a queued kernel descriptor alike.
- Blocking stays bounded and explicit. `gpio_wait` is the only call the
  facility adds that blocks, it takes a deadline, and it never returns
  early for any reason but an edge; an unrelated wake or an interrupted
  sleep is resumed. On Linux the thread sleeps in the kernel. On the Pico
  the core sleeps between edges when the SDK can arm an alarm for the
  deadline, and busy-waits to the deadline when it cannot; the SDK's
  `best_effort_wfe_or_timeout` chooses, and the answer is the same either
  way. Nothing else in the facility waits.
- The four calls run from ordinary application code. On the Pico they run
  on the core that made the watch, because the read-and-clear that makes
  `gpio_take` atomic against the handler masks that core's interrupts and
  only that core's; a call from the other core answers `Unsupported`. On
  Linux the calls are made from the program's thread, which the kernel may
  schedule on any core; the provider's atomicity is the kernel's, and no
  core rule applies.

The consumer the model is for is a device driver that keeps its synchronous
`read` and uses the latch only to skip work it can prove is unnecessary.
Take the touch controller: if its interrupt line falls when a report is
ready, as its datasheet says and the hardware check the plan requires must
confirm, then a held finger produces no further edge and a release is the
other edge, so a driver that read the controller only after a falling edge
would report a held finger as released, which changes what `read` means.
The shape that does not is: from a state the driver has verified as idle
-- its last bus read found no contact -- consult the latch, and skip the
bus while it is clear; from any other state -- contacts, an unsettled
report, a failure -- read the bus every time, as today. The latch decides
only whether an idle read touches the bus. Model 1's loop, cheaper, with
`read` meaning what it meant.

This is prospective. The CST328 driver polls the bus on every `read`
today, and docs/modules-touch.mdy says so.

# What is excluded

These are not omissions. Each is a shape that was considered and declined,
and the reason is recorded so it is not reconsidered by accident.

- Callback registration by portable code. A callback runs in whatever
  context the provider has, which on the Pico is an interrupt handler.
  docs/modules-c++20.mdy forbids `std::function` and manual allocation, so
  a stored callback would be a bare function pointer with a context the
  caller keeps alive; the device drivers hold state machines that are not
  re-entrant; and mm.mcu promises nothing about priorities or preemption.
  A callback contract would have to say all of that, and every driver that
  accepted one would have to be audited against it.
- General interrupt access. No portable interface names a vector, sets a
  priority, installs a handler, or receives timer, DMA, or bus interrupts.
- Preemption and threads above the seam. A provider MAY use a thread
  inside itself where its platform requires one; it MUST NOT run portable
  code on it, and it MUST NOT expose it.
- Signals as delivery. A provider MUST NOT install a process-wide signal
  handler for a portable facility. A Linux signal path for GPIO edges has
  been designed as a board-selected option behind the same four calls, and
  is deferred until something in the tree needs to be interrupted rather
  than to ask.

Two shapes would fit if a need appears, and both sit above the latch rather
than replacing it: a thread-mode dispatch that the loop calls and that
invokes a function pointer with a context for each pin whose latch is set,
and the signal option above.

# Conformance

A new interface, driver, provider, or application conforms when a reviewer
can answer yes to each of these against its document and its code.

- Every wait it makes on the caller's behalf is bounded by a value its
  wiring or configuration declares; reaching the bound returns, with
  `Timeout` if required work did not finish and `Ok` if the wait was the
  work.
- A call with nothing to report answers `Ok` with an empty result, and its
  document says so.
- No query waits for state to change.
- No call sleeps, spins, or blocks except `delay_ms`, a transfer, a
  declared bounded wait, and `gpio_wait`.
- Output parameters change only on `Ok`, the provider's record stays true
  after a failure, and its document says whether the hardware is left as
  it was.
- No handler, thread, or signal disposition exists outside a provider, and
  a provider's handler sets a flag and nothing else.
- Nothing the program can observe changes between two of its calls except
  as the hardware changes it.

The host tests are where the rules are pinned: each interface's recording
stand-in exercises the empty answer and the unchanged output on failure,
the provider tests exercise whatever hardware rollback an operation's
document promises, and the GPIO latch's tests exercise the forwarding and
the bound of the read step without hardware. Physical
timing -- that a bounded wait ends, that an edge is latched -- is a board
observation a person reads, as docs/modules-platform-mcu.mdy says of every
provider.

# What this does not settle

Whether a program may run on more than one core, and what the seams would
promise if it did. The Pico latch supports the core that made the watch,
and Linux has no core rule at all; nothing else in the tree has an
opinion. Whether a loop with nothing to do
should sleep the core by default; `gpio_wait` is the one call that does,
and only when asked. Whether a thread-mode dispatch is wanted at all, and
if so what a handler's context argument should be. Each is a change to
this document before it is a change to code.
